# FastDPS product roadmap

Status date: 2026-09-02

This file deliberately uses a portable text format despite the `.doc`
extension requested for the project. It can be read, diffed, and reviewed
without proprietary office software.

## Release 0.1 — implemented foundation

Status: engineering complete in the local repository.

- Chat-first responsive workspace with conversation history, a central chat,
  typed server-sent events, and a structured artifact panel.
- White public landing page, top-right sign-in, local accounts, passwordless
  opt-in demonstration access, and Google OpenID Connect configuration points.
- Multi-organisation membership and tenant-scoped access.
- Dynamic role names and rights with nine seeded roles, a fixed permission
  catalogue, role editing and assignment, expiring invitations, privilege
  grant boundaries, and last-owner protection.
- Configurable workflow-definition, step, and transition schema with default
  DPS, admission, and call-off workflows and administration API operations.
- Complete DPS lifecycle: systems, categories, qualification criteria,
  publication, closure, and archive states.
- Two-sided supplier lifecycle: supplier profiles, continuously open admission
  applications, reviews, admission decisions, suspensions, and call-off
  invitations.
- Competition lifecycle: submissions, weighted criteria, conflict declarations,
  evaluation scores, award approval/publication, and contract creation.
- Versioned, content-hashed document storage and download controls.
- Append-only audit events plus notification, comment, outbox, and idempotent
  webhook-receipt foundations.
- OCDS release/package import, canonical storage, searchable projection, DPS
  export, and optional pinned TED/Doffin eForms conversion.
- SQLite default, additive migrations, bounded PostgreSQL adapter and schema
  support, health endpoint, FastAPI/OpenAPI, Docker, Compose, and CI.
- Automated tests for migrations, lifecycle invariants, RBAC and tenant
  boundaries, invitation acceptance, chat confirmations, OCDS idempotency,
  document versions, CSRF, core pages, and API discovery.

## Release 0.2 — production hardening follow-up

These items require deployment credentials, external service selection, legal
policy input, or representative operational data and are therefore the next
follow-up rather than hidden local assumptions.

- Run and certify the optional PostgreSQL parity suite against the supplied
  managed database; add native indexes informed by real query plans.
- Configure the production domain, TLS, stable session secret, Google callback,
  identity allowlists, backup policy, encrypted persistent storage, and runtime
  observability.
- Add outbound invitation and notification delivery after an email provider is
  selected; process the existing transactional outbox with retry and dead-letter
  visibility.
- Add antivirus scanning, file-type inspection, retention, and object-storage
  adapters for production evidence.
- Add scheduled notice acquisition connectors after the exact TED/Doffin feeds,
  cadence, terms, and completeness expectations are confirmed. XML upload and
  conversion already work through the same ingestion boundary.
- Validate one optional EU-style policy profile with procurement counsel while
  retaining the jurisdiction-neutral core. Legal rules must remain versioned
  configuration rather than application constants.
- Expand workflow administration from API-first editing into a visual workflow
  designer with transition validation and impact previews.
- Add supplier co-worker invitations and granular supplier-side permissions.
- Add evaluation moderation, consensus scoring, moderation notes, standstill
  dates, notices, and contract-performance milestones.
- Add accessibility audit, localization framework, performance budgets, and
  independent security review.

## Release 0.3 — ecosystem and intelligence follow-up

- Background indexing and retrieval over approved procurement documents.
- Additional conversational tools for completeness checks, aggregate evaluation
  summaries, timeline risk, and OCDS data-quality explanations. Governed chat
  actions already cover DPS/category/criterion creation, publication, supplier
  decisions, competitions, scoring, awards, contracts, and notice search.
- Human-reviewed templates and policy packs distributed separately from the
  neutral core.
- Signed webhooks, SCIM, additional OIDC providers, and suite-to-suite API
  contracts.
- Public transparency views, release-package history, and configurable open-data
  publication endpoints.
- Operational dashboards for supplier participation, admission throughput,
  competition cycle time, savings, and data quality.

## Release gates

Each release requires passing tests, migration replay on fresh and upgraded
databases, tenant-isolation review, permission-matrix review, desktop and mobile
browser checks, API contract review, secret scanning, and documentation updates.
AI functionality cannot be promoted if it bypasses the same service, permission,
confirmation, and audit controls used by non-chat workflows.
